{"page_content": "6 \n \nwhich surrounds the droplet and lies between the droplet surface and the flame, became unstable and ill-\ndefined at higher pressures, a phenomenon which is not fully understood.  \nd. Organization of Data in PSI \nThe organization od the data posted in the PSI website is as follows: \n \n\u2022 \nPure n-dodecane data: FLEX-1206 to FLEX-1284 \n\u2022 \nN-dodecane/Iso-dodecane ( 75%-25% & 60%-40%) mixture data: FLEX-1285 to FLEX-1387) \n\u2022 \nFarnesane data: FLEX-1388 to FLEX-1431 \n \n5.  Results \na. Findings  \nAll the CFI data is yet to be fully analyzed and interpreted using theoretical and numerical models.  A brief \noverview of general observations made during preliminary data analysis prior to posting on the PSI website is \npresented below.  Interested researchers are welcome to propose to NASA for further analysis and \ninterpretation of CFI data. \n \n5.1 Cool flames: \nCool flames were observed during n-dodecanol combustion as well as n-dodecanol/iso-dodecanol mixture cases. \nThe addition of iso-dodecanol reduces the tendency to form cool flames in n-dodecanol.  Figure 1 shows a \ntypical case of cool-flame behavior for n-dodecanol.  \n \n \nFigure 1. FLEX-1223: N-dodecane at 1.25 atm pressure in air. \n \n5.2 Warm flames: \nWarm flames are a consequence of changing in chemical pathway during n-alkane oxidation.  Figure 2 shows \n(Westbrook, 2010) the various chemical pathways: cool flames occur when low-temperature branching is \ndominant, when intermediate temperatures become more active warm flames are established.  During warm \nflames the heat release rate dynamically balance the heat loss rate. Addition of helium seems to enhance this \nbalance within the NTC region.", "metadata": {"source": "CFI Science Final Report.pdf", "page": 7}}